---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Section from "../components/layout/Section.astro";
import Container from "../components/layout/Container.astro";
import Footer from "../components/layout/Footer.astro";
import ClassifiedBand from "../components/ui/ClassifiedBand.astro";
import PitchDeckNav from "../components/ui/PitchDeckNav.astro";
import CTA from "../components/ui/CTA.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../components/typography/Eyebrow.astro";
import Heading from "../components/typography/Heading.astro";
import Rule from "../components/typography/Rule.astro";
import PitchDeckLayout from "@/layouts/PitchDeckLayout.astro";
import "@/styles/global.css";

// @TODO: see if we can get any components or CSS out of this page
---

<PitchDeckLayout
  title="Tone & Aesthetics"
  eyebrow="Creative Briefing"
  classificationLabel="TOP SECRET — MI5 EXECUTIVE BRIEFING // AESTHETIC PROTOCOL"
  currentPath="/tone"
>
<p slot="header">
  The visual language of <em>Her Majesty's Displeasure</em> is a deliberate
  clash of two worlds, inspired by the work of Wes Anderson and Steven Conrad.
  It operates on a principle of <strong>Miserable Symmetry</strong>—the
  idea that the rigid, mathematical systems of the state will always
  attempt to iron out the beautiful, unpredictable wrinkles of human art,
  failing spectacularly in the process.
</p>

  <!-- ─── THE TWO WORLDS ─── -->

  <Section variant="dark" spacing="md">
  <div class="world-split">
    <!-- WORLD A: THE APPARATUS -->
    <div class="world-half world-half--apparatus">
      <!-- <Section spacing="md" variant="dark"> -->
        <Container>
          <Heading level={4} variant="section">World A: The Apparatus</Heading>
          <p class="subtitle">The Crown & MI5</p>
        </Container>
      <!-- </Section> -->

      <div class="world-content">
        <div class="world-image">
          <img
            src="/queen.jpg"
            alt="Buckingham Palace illustration"
            class="world-img"
          />
          <div class="world-image-overlay"></div>
          <div class="world-image-caption">
            The Apparatus — Institutional Power
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="prose">
          This is the domain of Prince Philip, Lord Mountbatten, and agents
          Nigel Finch and Clive Barrow. It is a world governed by inherited
          rank, stale tea, and outdated paperwork.
        </p>

        <div class="visual-principles">
          <h4>The Look</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Mathematically precise, deadpan, and wide. The camera remains
            entirely static, trapped on a tripod or a heavy dolly grip, evoking
            the sterile, locked-down surveillance language of Steven Conrad's <em
              >Patriot</em
            >.
          </p>

          <h4>The Framing</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Extreme, rigid symmetry reminiscent of Wes Anderson. Characters are
            frequently placed dead-center in oversized, cavernous
            frames—emphasizing how small and inconsequential these "powerful"
            middle-managers actually are against the backdrop of their fading
            empire.
          </p>

          <h4>The Palette</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Completely desaturated. Muted greys, institutional pale yellows,
            dusty file-folder manila, and deep navy blue. The only sharp pop of
            color is the violent, wet red of a government top-secret ink stamp.
          </p>

          <h4>The Spaces</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Sterile, dusty, and silent. Massive, empty Buckingham Palace sitting
            rooms and cramped, windowless MI5 basement offices lit by
            flickering, harsh fluorescent bars.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- WORLD B: THE FREQUENCY -->
    <div class="world-half world-half--frequency">
      <!-- <Section variant="black" spacing="md"> -->
        <Container>
          <Heading level={4} variant="section">World B: The Frequency</Heading>
          <p class="subtitle">The Beatles & Abbey Road</p>
        </Container>
      <!-- </Section> -->

      <div class="world-content">
        <div class="world-image">
          <img
            src="/thebeatles01.jpg"
            alt="The Beatles recording"
            class="world-img"
          />
          <div class="world-image-overlay"></div>
          <div class="world-image-caption">The Frequency — Creative Chaos</div>
        </div>

        <p class="prose">
          This is the domain of the band—cluttered, fluid, chemically altered,
          and intensely collaborative.
        </p>

        <div class="visual-principles">
          <h4>The Look</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Handheld, intimate, organic, and reactive. The camera breathes with
            the musicians, floating around the studio floor to capture the
            accidental lightning-in-a-bottle nature of their songwriting.
          </p>

          <h4>The Framing</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Asymmetrical, crowded, and alive. Frames are packed with
            instruments, half-empty teacups, cigarette smoke, lyric sheets, and
            people.
          </p>

          <h4>The Palette</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Rich, warm, and progressive. It begins in 1965 with deep, saturated
            mahogany wood tones and autumn corduroys, steadily evolving by 1969
            into a psychedelic tapestry of counter-culture color—matching John
            Lennon's radicalization.
          </p>

          <h4>The Spaces</h4>
          <p class="prose">
            Textured and vibrating. The smoke-filled, claustrophobic air of
            Abbey Road Studio Two, littered with tape reels and amplifiers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
  <!-- ─── TIMELINE & CULTURAL SHIFT ─── -->
  <Section variant="paper" spacing="md">
    <Container>
      <Eyebrow>Historical Context</Eyebrow>
      <Heading level={3} variant="section">The 1965–1970 Timeline</Heading>
      <Rule />
      <p class="prose">
        This section establishes the show's timeline and explains how it tracks
        the massive cultural shift of the late 60s—moving from the clean-cut,
        polite MBE era to the raw, radical anti-war movement. The visual
        contrast between the two worlds mirrors the societal transformation
        occurring in Britain and the West.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </Section>
</PitchDeckLayout>

<style>
  /* ─── WORLD IMAGE ─── */
  .world-image {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 40vh;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }

  .world-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    transition: transform 0.3s ease;
  }

  .world-image:hover .world-img {
    transform: scale(1.03);
  }

  .world-image-overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      transparent 50%,
      rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3) 80%
    );
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .world-image-caption {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    padding: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs0);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    background: var(--bg-subtle);
  }

  /* ─── WORLD SPLIT ─── */
  .world-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    min-height: 80vh;
  }

  .world-half {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--space-5);
  }

  .world-half--apparatus {
    background: var(--bg);
    border-right: 2px solid var(--text-muted);
  }

  .world-half--frequency {
    background: var(--orange6);
  }

  .subtitle {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs3);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.15rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-blue);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0 0;
  }

  .world-content {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .world-content p {
    font-size: var(--fs2);
  }
  .visual-principles {
    /* margin-top: var(--space-5); */
  }

  .visual-principles h4 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs2);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--red-text5);
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-2);
  }

  .visual-principles p {
    font-size: var(--fs3);
  }

  /* ─── RESPONSIVE ─── */
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .world-split {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      min-height: auto;
    }

    .world-half {
      min-height: auto;
    }

    .world-content {
      margin-top: var(--space-3);
    }

    .world-image {
      height: 30vh;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .world-image {
      height: 25vh;
    }
  }
</style>
